  "decision_summary": "Prediction high: L1/L2 gold-context records from structurally related small polar polyhydroxy compounds (mannitol, glucose, propylene glycol, sorbitol in shunted/cirrhotic patients) show oral bioavailability at or above 20%, and L3 oral exposure records for mannitol confirm measurable systemic exposure of very polar small molecules after oral dosing. L4 adds high aqueous solubility for related polar molecules and shows that mannitol's low Caco-2 permeability coexists with measured F 20-63%, so low passive permeability is not threshold-relevant negative for ascorbic acid, which has active transport noted in the query prior. L5 adds bidirectional transport records showing no gut-wall efflux for mannitol (efflux ratios near unity), providing mechanistic support against presystemic efflux loss, though no ascorbic acid-specific intestinal metabolism/efflux record is present. Low-F records are explained by first-pass metabolism/clearance or species-specific presystemic catabolism not applicable to ascorbic acid. The physicochemical prior is uncertain and does not outweigh this empirical pattern."
}

